      "content": "You are right to identify \"stability\" as a frequent pretext for censorship, as international human rights law generally prohibits the total shutdown of the internet during public protests unless there is an immediate threat to life. The core of this debate lies in the distinction between reasonable, time-limited security measures and the overuse of broad suspension powers to silence dissent. Consequently, legal frameworks in many democratic nations strictly limit such actions to extreme emergency scenarios and require robust judicial oversight.",
      "source": "target",
